n/id #uuid "9e6af293-22e2-57dc-ac36-0c68460f20c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-137095" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ -14 & 14 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4a+\\frac{b}{c}=4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{1}=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-137095/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-137095"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5963"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/86e05af9d6062530450e56f5e292106413301a869e765f413dcbe6865a6ce8a5.png)\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/c2daef6c8fee0dd47562395ec76a2912eaea98e3a873d6319553ed281745d210.png)\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}80 & -60 & -20 \\\\ -60 & 45 & 15 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 5\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=(-20)-(-24)+(-20) = -16.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5590"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/98b9eaa366bb0c1dcf899eb231b34e9704335a294d4b69c6af10197eb14929ce.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/b36e5a28c59c1ab0be811fb94fec4dc018b6687229f4acb3ef11a745fdeb6631.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5591"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/8803a165894746f62c8d27530eebd791d03cfc16ee8da0dd3e176c30a400ee4a.png)\n\nThe least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/1f35d807d723ae25214ce1cdd9fdb42c5111f6a0495a5f453e8a04b415863466.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
